  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Chances for rain Sunday. Precipitation chances increasing \nfor Wednesday through Thanksgiving.\n\nHigh pressure currently over the region today will slowly\nretreat to the north and east through the remainder of today and\non Saturday. This retreat is partially due to a\nnorth/northeastward progressing surface low pressure system \ndeveloping along an east/west frontal boundary along the Florida\nPeninsula. This wave of low pressure looks to bring light rain \nshowers to the region starting Saturday night into Sunday \nmorning; however, it will likely take time for the dry air in \nplace over the region to become eroded by the increase in \nmoisture leading to a possible delayed start leaning towards\nSunday morning. \n\nSome uncertainty remains in total rainfall amounts forecast \nthrough Monday morning, as the mean for the ECMWF ensembles has \nbetween 0.25-0.50\" of rainfall for much of the area. Meanwhile, \nthe GFS ensemble mean has between 0.50-0.75\" of rainfall for \nmuch of the area. Overall, the areas that seem to be favored are\nalong and east of highway 29 between Lynchburg and Danville. \nThe 72 hour WPC 1 in 10 chance currently runs rainfall totals \nbetween 1.5-3.0\", meanwhile the 9 in 10 chance of higher amounts\nhas much of the area near 0.0\" of rainfall. Given this large \nspread, there is still large variability, and some areas may see\nslightly higher or lower totals depending on several factors. \n\nThe reason for this variability looks to remain partially in the\ndevelopment of the surface low pressure system, and the timing\nof the shortwave trough that looks to pull the low to the\nnortheast as it reaches the Carolina coastline. Regardless, any\nrain in our region would be beneficial given the persistent \ndrought conditions across southern VA and eastern NC. \n\nThe aforementioned upper level trough looks to bring a cold\nfront into the region late in the weekend, with high pressure\nlooking to settle across the Northeast in its wake. This is\nexpected to lead to strong wedging settling into the region\nstarting late Tuesday. Both the ECMWF and GFS have upper level\ntroughs moving into the eastern Conus by late Wednesday into\nThursday. This may lead to increased rainfall chances heading\ninto the Thanksgiving holiday across the region. With the strong\nwedging pattern setting up over the region heading into\nWednesday and Thursday, and moisture likely being advected over\nthe top of it, there is a chance for rain and possibly freezing\nrain to occur Wednesday and Thursday. In all likelihood, any\nwintry precipitation would be relegated to higher terrain\nlocations and mountain ridge lines, and while specific impacts \nto the region currently still remain uncertain at this time, \nthis system is one to watch as it may lead to travel delays \ngiven its proximity in timing to the Thanksgiving holiday. \nOverall, the larger scale pattern favors a wet, chilly, and \nsomewhat windy thanksgiving holiday for the area.",
